*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !is_grp_member(user,group)
*
*    user - Windows user name to test for membership of a Windows local group
*
*    This function sets the STATUS() value to 0 if successful, 1 if the
*    specified group does not exist.
*
*    WHY THIS NO LONGER READS /etc/group.  It used to open /etc and read
*    "group" as a text file.  MSYS2 and Cygwin dropped /etc/passwd and
*    /etc/group in favour of direct SAM lookups, so the open failed, the
*    routine set status 1 and returned false for EVERYONE, and the sdusers
*    test at LOGIN terminated every connection with "This user is not
*    registered for SD use".  Nothing about it looked like a missing file.
*    See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 6.
*
*    Get-LocalGroupMember is asked instead, and it distinguishes the three
*    answers the caller needs, which parsing "net localgroup" output does not
*    do without depending on the language Windows is installed in:
*
*       exit 0   a member          -> @true,  status 0
*       exit 1   not a member      -> @false, status 0
*       exit 2   no such group     -> @false, status 1
*
*    Membership is compared on the last backslash-delimited component, so
*    MACHINE\don and don both match don.
*
*    COST TO KNOW ABOUT: this spawns bash and then PowerShell, which is slow
*    enough to notice on a path that runs at every login.  The fast answer is
*    getgrnam() behind a KERNEL key - it is already known to resolve Windows
*    local groups correctly (PROJECT_STATUS.md section 4) - but that is new C
*    code and this is not.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function is_grp_member(user,group)
$catalog !is_grp_member

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

user_found = @false
set.status 0

* Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
* Empty user or group cannot match membership; skip the lookup.
if user = '' or group = '' then
   set.status 1
   return user_found
end
* --------------------

* rev 0.9.0  is this the user's own group account?
if upcase(user) = upcase(group) then return @true

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - both names are interpolated into a shell command
*   below, so they are validated first.  !valid_os_name permits letters,
*   digits, dot, underscore and hyphen only, which is what makes the quoting
*   below sufficient rather than merely tidy.
if not(valid_os_name(user)) or not(valid_os_name(group)) then
   set.status 1
   return user_found
end

   q = char(39)   ;* apostrophe - bash quoting, see below

* The script is wrapped in single quotes for bash, which makes every $ and
* backslash in it literal, and uses double quotes for its own strings.  An
* apostrophe cannot appear in either name, so the quoting cannot be escaped.
* Output is discarded: this runs at login and PowerShell's error text is not
* the user's business.
   ps = 'try { $ms = Get-LocalGroupMember -Group "' : group
   ps := '" -ErrorAction Stop } catch { exit 2 }; '
   ps := 'foreach ($m in $ms) { if ((($m.Name -split "\\")[-1]) -ieq "'
   ps := user : '") { exit 0 } }; exit 1'

   os.execute 'powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive -Command ' : q : ps : q : ' >/dev/null 2>&1'

   stat = os.error()

   begin case
      case stat = 0
         user_found = @true
      case stat = 1
         user_found = @false
      case 1
         * no such group, or PowerShell could not be run at all
         set.status 1
   end case

return user_found
end

* END-CODE
